---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GovernedAITransaction from "../components/diagrams/GovernedAITransaction.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Canonical Workflow | Complete Governed AI Transaction"
  description="BeaconGuard’s complete governed AI transaction: Request Admission, provider invocation, untrusted AI result, Result-to-Action Authorization, enterprise execution, system-of-record effect, and verification."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Complete governed AI transaction</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      BeaconGuard authorizes whether an enterprise application may invoke an AI
      provider and whether a declared enterprise action based on the returned
      result may proceed. AI output remains an untrusted proposal. BeaconGuard
      authorizes; the enterprise application executes; systems of record remain
      authoritative.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Shared governed transaction sequence</h2>
    <p>
      The complete sequence separates provider invocation from enterprise
      effect. Request Admission ALLOW permits the provider call. Enterprise
      effect still requires independent Result-to-Action Authorization of the
      declared intended action.
    </p>
    <GovernedAITransaction />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Separated stages</h2>
    <div class="scenario-grid">
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Provider invocation</h3>
        <p>
          After Request Admission ALLOW, the enterprise application may invoke
          the AI provider under the Active Verified Release. DENY blocks
          invocation and records the decision.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Provider result</h3>
        <p>
          The AI-generated result is an untrusted proposal. Provider success is
          not enterprise authorization and does not grant permission to change
          enterprise state.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Enterprise action</h3>
        <p>
          The enterprise application declares the intended enterprise action —
          for example, a communication, documentation update, escalation, or
          case-state change — and submits it for Result-to-Action Authorization.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Enterprise effect</h3>
        <p>
          Only after action ALLOW may the application execute the authorized
          action. DENY hard-stops enterprise effect. The system of record
          remains authoritative for resulting business state.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Verification</h3>
        <p>
          Related Verifiable Decision Records support investigation through
          BeaconGuard Verify: reporting, Decision Explorer, reconstruction,
          source evidence, replay verification, and verifiable export. Verify is
          read-only and cannot grant runtime authority.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Industry context panels</h2>
    <p>
      The same governed transaction applies across industries. What changes is
      the source application, required context, declared actions, and review
      stakeholders — not the dual authorization model.
    </p>
    <div class="scenario-grid">
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Healthcare</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Typical source application:</strong> Care operations,
          privacy-gated assist, or EHR-adjacent workflow UI.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Required context:</strong> Role, workflow identity, approved
          pathway, source-system trust, context tags, freshness.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Example declared actions:</strong> Patient communication,
          documentation draft use, care-operations routing or status update.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Authoritative system:</strong> EHR and clinical operations.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Review stakeholders:</strong> Privacy, compliance, clinical
          governance, security, architecture.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Financial</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Typical source application:</strong> AML, KYC, fraud, or
          dispute analyst workflow.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Required context:</strong> Analyst or service identity, case
          and transaction context, source trust, requested action, freshness.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Example declared actions:</strong> Case recommendation,
          escalation, customer communication, case-state change, fraud or
          dispute action.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Authoritative system:</strong> Case and transaction platforms.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Review stakeholders:</strong> Risk, compliance, fraud
          operations, security, audit.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Regulated Internal Automation</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Typical source application:</strong> Existing internal agent
          or automation orchestrator.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Required context:</strong> Role, environment, requested
          action, change window, freshness.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Example declared actions:</strong> Governed automation steps
          against production targets after independent action authorization.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Authoritative system:</strong> Production systems of record.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Review stakeholders:</strong> Security, operations, risk,
          audit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Decision evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Admission and action decisions produce related Verifiable Decision
      Records. Reviewers inspect the decision signal, authorization point,
      request metadata, release identity, freshness and replay checks, result
      binding where applicable, and declared intended action — without treating
      review as a permission result.
    </p>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/proof-evidence">Review Decision Evidence</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/healthcare">Healthcare Use Case</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/fintech">Financial Use Case</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
